"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nby reference). Importantly, the additional terms must be accessible by the consumer before the\nconsumer clicks \"I Agree.\" See State ex rel. U-Haul Co. v. Zakaib, 752 S.E.2d 586 (W. Va. 2013)\n(holding that business was unsuccessful in its attempts to incorporate an addendum because it was\nonly provided to consumers after a rental agreement was executed). The reasonableness of the\nnotice depends on how likely the terms are to come to the attention of a reasonably prudent user\nin that particular context. See Sarchi v. Uber Techs., Inc., 268 A.3d 258, 268 (Me. 2022) (stating\nthat \"[t]he first step focuses on whether a user had reasonable notice of the online contract terms\n'consider[ing] the perspective of a reasonably prudent ... user' of online technology\") (quoting\nMeyer v. Uber Techs., Inc., 868 F.3d 66, 77 (2d Cir. 2017)). See also, e.g., Kauders v. Uber Techs.,\nInc., 159 N.E.3d 1033 (Mass. 2021); Cullinane v. Uber Techs., Inc., 893 F.3d 53, 61 (1st Cir.\n2018); Meyer v. Uber Techs., 868 F.3d 66 (2d Cir. 2017).\nA traditional analysis focusing exclusively on all the decisions by state supreme and\nappellate courts through 2022 reveals that courts routinely hold that the clickwrap method is\neffective in adopting the terms, as long as a clear notice alerts the consumer that such terms are\nintended to be part of the contract. In all supreme-court or appellate-court decisions on point,\nclickwraps accompanied by proper notice were deemed to be an effective method of adoption of\nterms. The supreme courts of five states-Louisiana, Maine, Massachusetts, Washington, and\nWest Virginia-have ruled specifically on the enforceability of clickwraps. Eight additional states\nhave opinions on point written by state appellate courts. In the absence of decisions on point by\nstate supreme or appellate courts, other Restatements have consulted opinions written by federal\ncircuit courts interpreting state law. That approach is followed throughout this Restatement. The\n19 high state-court decisions include Adsit Co., Inc. v. Gustin, 874 N.E.2d 1018 (Ind. Ct. App.\n2007) (Indiana: enforcing a forum-selection clause when consumers were asked to click an\n\"I Accept\" button to manifest assent to the terms, the terms were short and clear, and the forum-\nselection provision was clearly marked in bold and capital letters); Jallali v. Nat'l Bd. of\nOsteopathic Med. Exam'rs, Inc., 908 N.E.2d 1168 (Ind. Ct. App. 2009) (Indiana: finding the\nforum-selection clause and confidentiality clauses in a clickwrap agreement enforceable); Moore\nv. Microsoft Corp., 741 N.Y.S.2d 91, 92 (N.Y. App. Div. 2002) (New York: ruling that a validly\nbinding contract was formed when user indicated assent to the end-user license agreement by\nclicking on an icon before proceeding with the download of the software); Hodges v. Condors\nSwim Club of Clarkstown, Inc., 986 N.Y.S.2d 865 (Table), 2014 WL 553288 (N.Y. App. Div.\n2014) (New York: holding that online terms that are \"electronically signed\" when registering for\na service are enforceable, and noting the terms at issue were clear); Durrett v. ACT, Inc., 310 P.3d\n1047 (Haw. Ct. App. 2011) (Hawaii: concluding that an agreement with an arbitration provision\nwas binding because plaintiff provided explicit assent by electronically checking the box);\nSwanson v. U-Haul Int'l, Inc., 2014 WL 1673115 (Ill. App. Ct. Apr. 23, 2014) (Illinois: affirming\ndefendants' motion to compel arbitration, when plaintiffs had clicked to accept the terms and\nconditions of rental contract with a valid arbitration provision); Bonck v. White, 115 So. 3d 651\n(La. Ct. App. 2013) (Louisiana: reversing summary judgment because of the existence of genuine\nissues of material fact, but finding that electronic signatures are acceptable under Louisiana law\n61\n@§2\n"